---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import MetricsStrip from "../components/MetricsStrip.astro";
import CaveatCard from "../components/CaveatCard.astro";
import A3Backend from "../components/figures/A3Backend.astro";
import A4Kleidi from "../components/figures/A4Kleidi.astro";
import A5Context from "../components/figures/A5Context.astro";
import A6Thermal from "../components/figures/A6Thermal.astro";
import A7Phases from "../components/figures/A7Phases.astro";
import { withBase } from "../lib/url";
---

<Layout
  title="Optimizations - ENTITY"
  description="Every Arm-specific runtime lever ENTITY ships - backend dispatch, core affinity, thread derivation, adaptive context, thermal guard, energy telemetry, and a model-free device probe that predicts throughput before a download - each with what it measured and where it lives in the source."
  path="/optimizations/"
>
  <section data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 pt-12 pb-8">
    <p class="microlabel">docs/OPTIMIZATIONS.md &middot; the app's ABOUT screen</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold">Every lever, with sources</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-dim text-[0.875rem]">
      What each runtime decision does, why it works, what it measured, and where it is in the
      source. Negative results are kept at equal weight - two of these levers were regressions
      and were removed or demoted, and that is stated here, not buried.
    </p>
  </section>

  <MetricsStrip />

  <section data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 py-10 space-y-10">
    <!-- 1. backend dispatch -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,20rem)_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-6 items-start">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Runtime-selected CPU backend</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.8125rem] text-dim leading-relaxed">
          The build compiles seven Arm CPU backend variants, armv8.0 through armv9.2, each with
          Arm KleidiAI micro-kernels, via stock llama.cpp <code>GGML_CPU_ALL_VARIANTS=ON</code> -
          a build decision, not hand-written kernels. At startup
          <code>ggml_backend_load_all_from_path</code> loads every variant in the APK and ggml
          scores them against the physical CPU; the best supported one runs. Old phones without
          dotprod get the armv8.0 fallback instead of SIGILL.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.75rem] text-dim">
          Source: <code>lib/build.gradle.kts</code>, <code>ai_chat.cpp: init()</code>. Cost:
          release APK ~9.8 MB against ~7 MB single-variant.
        </p>
      </div>
      <A3Backend />
    </div>

    <!-- 2. affinity -->
    <div class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Big-core affinity</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed">
        <div class="text-dim space-y-2">
          <p>
            <code>build_fast_cpu_set()</code> ranks every online core by
            <code>cpuinfo_max_freq</code> - live hardware detection, not a hardcoded mask - and
            <code>pin_to_fast_cores()</code> applies it with <code>sched_setaffinity</code> on
            every decode entry point, because ggml's worker pool spawns lazily and inherits the
            calling thread's mask. Each benchmark arm logs the mask the kernel actually applied,
            so a failed pin cannot pass as "pinning earns nothing".
          </p>
          <p>
            Source: <code>ai_chat.cpp: build_fast_cpu_set / pin_to_fast_cores</code>; the
            ablation switch is <code>g_pin_cores</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-2">
          <p>
            <span class="font-bold">Measured:</span> on the five-run 2026-07-18 exports, pinning
            over the same thread count earns <span class="font-bold">+21% decode</span> on the
            Dimensity 7300 (distributions non-overlapping) and
            <span class="font-bold">+1% decode but median power 2.52 &rarr; 1.78 W</span> on the
            Snapdragon 6 Gen 4. July's three-run sets read ~0%. Across the contributed dataset the
            median is +0.7% decode and +2.0% tok/W, over a range of -8.5% to +29.3% - so
            device-dependent that v3.5.0 made placement a
            <a href={withBase("/apps/")}>user setting</a> rather than ship any default as correct.
          </p>
          <p class="text-dim">
            The mechanism figure is <a href={withBase("/#a2")}>A2 on the overview</a>; the full
            pinning story is <a href={withBase("/leaderboard/#a8")}>A8 beside the leaderboard</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 3. thread derivation -->
    <div class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Adaptive thread count - two widths, two signals</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed">
        <div class="space-y-2">
          <p>
            <span class="font-bold">Decode</span> derives its width from the top frequency
            cluster: the count of cores within 10% of the fastest clock, clamped to 2-6. On the
            reference 4+4 that yields 4. Decode does not want width - an SM8550 in the dataset
            decodes <span class="font-bold">23.8 tok/s on 2 threads and 6.72 on 8</span> -
            because it is bandwidth-bound and two threads already saturate the bus.
          </p>
          <p>
            <span class="font-bold">Prefill</span> (since v3.5.0) derives a separate, usually
            wider set from the kernel's own per-core <code>cpu_capacity</code> (1024 = strongest):
            every core strictly above the slowest tier, never narrower than decode. Frequency
            cannot rank cores - an A55 at 2.0 GHz and an A78 at 2.5 GHz are 25% apart in clock
            and roughly 3x apart in throughput.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="text-dim space-y-2">
          <p>
            Why two rules? The single clock rule was validated on the devices actually measured -
            and the contributed dataset then proved it wrong for prefill on prime-core flagships,
            where the 10% window admits only the prime core and the count collapsed to 2. The
            decode rule was deliberately left alone; the prefill rule moved to capacity. The full
            falsification is on the <a href={withBase("/leaderboard/")}>leaderboard page</a> and
            in <a href={withBase("/journey/")}>the journey</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Source: <code>ai_chat.cpp: top_cluster_core_count / prompt_thread_count</code>,
            mirrored by <code>BenchmarkActivity.autoGenThreads()</code> - one definition, called
            from both places, after a drift bug proved why (Journey section 4).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 4. adaptive context -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,20rem)_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-6 items-start">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Adaptive context</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.8125rem] text-dim leading-relaxed">
          Auto mode buckets on GGUF file size and current free RAM to choose a 2048, 4096 or
          8192-token window before the model loads. Because weights are memory-mapped and paged
          on demand, the KV cache is the real RAM-pressure lever - which is what lets a 3B-class
          model run inside about 2 GB free instead of OOMing.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.75rem] text-dim">
          Source: <code>MainActivity.adaptiveContext()</code>,
          <code>ai_chat.cpp: init_context()</code> (reads back what llama.cpp actually
          allocated).
        </p>
      </div>
      <A5Context />
    </div>

    <!-- 5. thermal -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,20rem)_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-6 items-start">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Thermal-aware guard</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.8125rem] text-dim leading-relaxed">
          Every eighth generated token, Auto mode asks Android's thermal status (cached for one
          second) and inserts a cooperative delay: 0 ms at NONE or LIGHT, 6 ms at MODERATE,
          12 ms at SEVERE and above. Efficiency mode doubles the delay and caps inference at two
          threads. It is a soft back-off - no affinity change, no realtime request, no root-only
          thermal zones.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.75rem] text-dim">
          Source: <code>ChatViewModel.ThermalGuard.delayMs()</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <A6Thermal />
    </div>

    <!-- 6-8 small levers -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h2 class="text-[0.9375rem] font-bold">KV session cache</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.8125rem] text-dim leading-relaxed">
          Each conversation's decoded state is rebuilt on demand: switching to or restoring a
          persisted chat primes the native KV through the same chat-template path as live turns
          ("Preparing conversation..."), so the next reply continues seamlessly instead of
          starting from a cold cache. Long conversations never hard-stop: on overflow the oldest
          half of the turns is trimmed and the system prompt always survives.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.75rem] text-dim">
          Source: <code>ai_chat.cpp: primeHistoryNative / shift_context</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h2 class="text-[0.9375rem] font-bold">mmap weights</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.8125rem] text-dim leading-relaxed">
          GGUF weights are memory-mapped, paged in on demand rather than fully resident. RAM
          pressure therefore comes from the KV cache, which the adaptive-context rule sizes -
          the pairing that lets a 773 MB model report 1.2 GB free while generating.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.75rem] text-dim">Source: llama.cpp mmap loading, used as shipped.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h2 class="text-[0.9375rem] font-bold">Energy telemetry</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.8125rem] text-dim leading-relaxed">
          watts = |battery current| x voltage / 1e9, sampled every 150 ms during benchmark passes
          and once per render tick in chat. Both OEM current units are disambiguated by a
          plausibility window, and since v3.6.0 the voltage unit is normalised first - a
          contributed device reported whole volts where millivolts are documented and broke watts
          by a factor of a million. Power and tok/W hide entirely while charging.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[0.75rem] text-dim">
          Source: <code>MainActivity.snapMetrics()</code>, <code>PowerMath</code>,
          <code>BenchmarkActivity</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 9. device probe -->
    <div class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Predicting a model before downloading it</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed">
        <div class="text-dim space-y-2">
          <p>
            The catalog used to rank models on RAM and ISA flags, which answers "will it fit" and
            not "will it be usable". A 4 GB phone could be told a 3B model fits and still find it
            decodes at 3 tok/s - after a 2 GB download. That is the worst possible way to learn it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>DeviceProbe</code> answers it in about 60 ms with no model present, because the
            two phases of inference are bound by things that can be measured directly.
            <span class="font-bold">Decode reads essentially every weight once per token</span>, so
            <code>tok/s ~= bandwidth / model_bytes</code> - a physical relationship, not a fitted
            curve. It is measured with a 32 MB <code>arraycopy</code>, large enough that no phone's
            last-level cache can hold it, so the number is DRAM rather than SRAM.
            <span class="font-bold">Prefill is a GEMM over the whole prompt</span> and tracks
            integer throughput on the performance cores, measured with a dependency-chained
            multiply-accumulate loop - integer rather than float on purpose, since a float
            benchmark would rank a phone with strong FP and weak integer units far too highly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both readings are scaled against one calibration device measured properly with
            <code>llama-bench</code>, which is also what lets the estimate carry the Q4_0/Q8_0
            trade instead of treating "reaches KleidiAI" as a single good thing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Source: <code>DeviceProbe.kt</code>, <code>ModelCatalog.assess</code>. Anchor: CMF
            Phone 1, Dimensity 7300, Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct, 4 threads pinned.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-2">
          <p>
            <span class="font-bold">Sized against free memory, not installed RAM.</span> A 6 GB
            phone with a browser and a few background apps resident has well under 2 GB to give,
            and a rule written against 6 GB recommends models into memory that is already spoken
            for. The thresholds were re-derived rather than reused - fractions calibrated against
            total would reject models that genuinely run. A model is flagged tight above 70% of
            free memory rather than rejected, because weights are mmap'd page cache the kernel can
            evict; the KV cache is the anonymous memory that actually has to fit, and it is the
            smaller of the two. The cost is stated plainly on screen: the verdict moves with
            whatever else is running, because the phone really does have less room when it is busy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <span class="font-bold">This is an estimate and says so.</span> It cannot see thermal
            headroom, memory pressure from other apps, or the vendor's scheduler. It exists to keep
            a user off a model their phone cannot serve, not to replace a benchmark - the real
            three-arm ablation still ships in both apps.
          </p>
          <p class="text-dim">
            Two of its anchor constants were originally derived rather than measured, and were
            wrong by 4.1x and 2.5x. That is
            <a href={withBase("/journey/")}>entry 10 on the journey page</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ADPF -->
    <CaveatCard title="Shipped to be measured, not assumed: ADPF deadline hints (v3.6.0)">
      <p>
        Affinity controls where work runs, not how urgent it is. The one sanctioned route into
        the kernel's frequency machinery for an unprivileged app is an Android performance-hint
        session: ENTITY declares each decode step's target duration and reports the actual, and
        the platform adjusts clocks and placement with the vendor's own tuning.
        <strong>No speed or energy claim is attached to it yet</strong> - ENTITY Bench ships an
        <code>adpf</code> arm so the hint can be measured against threads-only and against the
        hard mask, and until devices report back it is documented as a mechanism, not a result.
      </p>
    </CaveatCard>
  </section>

  <MetricsStrip />

  <section data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 py-10 space-y-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold">The quantization gate, and why decode does not care</h2>
    <A4Kleidi />
    <A7Phases />
  </section>

  <section data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 py-10 space-y-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold">A file named Q4_0 is only 76% Q4_0</h2>
    <p class="text-dim max-w-[68ch] text-[0.875rem]">
      The gate above is a claim about <em>types</em>. Checking it properly meant reading each
      file's tensor table instead of its <code>general.file_type</code> label — and the label is
      wrong about its own contents. In the exact Llama-3.2-1B Q4_0 the catalog ships,
      <code>token_embd.weight</code> is Q6_K and two <code>ffn_down</code> tensors are Q4_1.
      Llama 3.2 ties its embeddings, so that Q6_K tensor is also the output projection: the
      largest matmul in the model sits off the fast path, in a quantization chosen because it
      reaches the fast path.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-[0.8125rem] border-collapse">
        <caption class="sr-only">Quality, size, KleidiAI coverage and measured speed by quantization</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--outline)] text-left">
            <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-bold">Quant</th>
            <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-right">Perplexity</th>
            <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-right">vs F16</th>
            <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-right">Size</th>
            <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-right">On KleidiAI</th>
            <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-right">Prefill</th>
            <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-bold text-right">Decode</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="font-mono">
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--outline)]">
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">F16 reference</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">14.2580</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">—</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">2480 MB</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">n/a</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">—</td><td class="py-2 text-right">—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--outline)]">
            <td class="py-2 pr-4"><strong>Q8_0</strong></td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">14.2705</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">+0.09%</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">1321 MB</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right"><strong>100%</strong></td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right"><strong>208.1</strong></td><td class="py-2 text-right">11.5</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--outline)]">
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">Q4_K_M</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">14.7346</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">+3.34%</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">808 MB</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">0%</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">111.7</td><td class="py-2 text-right">16.0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--outline)]">
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">Q4_0 imatrix <span class="text-dim">(shipped)</span></td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">15.6159</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">+9.52%</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">773 MB</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">76%</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">128.2</td><td class="py-2 text-right">18.2</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--outline)]">
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">Q3_K_L</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">16.0927</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">+12.87%</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">733 MB</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">0%</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">43.4</td><td class="py-2 text-right">17.4</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">Q4_0 no imatrix</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">16.5272</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">+15.92%</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">771 MB</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">79%</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right"><strong>138.8</strong></td><td class="py-2 text-right"><strong>19.1</strong></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-dim max-w-[68ch] text-[0.8125rem]">
      Perplexity: wikitext-2 test, 200 chunks, every quant built from one F16 source. Speed: CMF
      Phone 1, four threads pinned to the A78 cluster, three repetitions, 90 s cooldown between
      models. Coverage read from each file's tensor table, not its label.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <CaveatCard title="The number this project owed">
        <p>
          "Q4_0 is a quality tradeoff" appeared three times in these docs with no figure attached.
          It is <strong>5.6% perplexity against Q4_K_M</strong> — the price of the 3.4x cut in
          time-to-first-token. And a plain <code>llama-quantize … Q4_0</code>, the documented
          command, is <strong>5.5% worse</strong> than the imatrix-calibrated file the catalog
          ships, under the same filename and the same <code>file_type</code>. Nothing warns you.
        </p>
      </CaveatCard>
      <CaveatCard title="The imatrix costs speed as well as buying quality">
        <p>
          Naive Q4_0 is <em>faster</em> than the imatrix Q4_0 — 138.8 against 128.2 tok/s prefill,
          19.1 against 18.2 decode. The importance matrix promotes the first
          <code>ffn_down</code> layers to Q4_1, off the KleidiAI path, and adds 2 MB. So one flag
          buys 5.5% quality and costs 7.7% prefill, and neither effect is visible from outside
          the file.
        </p>
      </CaveatCard>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 py-10">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold">Negative results, at equal weight</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dim max-w-[68ch] text-[0.875rem]">
      Two of the ideas above shipped, measured slower, and were removed or demoted. They stay on
      this page at the same size as the wins.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <CaveatCard title="Regression: widening prompt processing to all cores">
        <p>
          Until v2.1.0, prompt processing spread across every online core, on the textbook
          assumption that a compute-bound phase wants all the hardware. Measured on the reference
          4+4: <strong>135 tok/s on the 4 fast cores, 86 tok/s across all 8</strong>. An A55 is
          roughly a third of an A78, so every GEMM waited on the stragglers. Removed in v2.1.0;
          the right width is an empirical property of the SoC, which is why the benchmark decides
          it rather than an assumption.
        </p>
      </CaveatCard>
      <CaveatCard title="Disproved: more KleidiAI coverage would be faster">
        <p>
          Given the 76% figure above, the obvious fix was to promote the output projection to
          Q8_0 — still an eligible type — taking coverage to <strong>97%</strong> for 8% more
          file and no quality change. The prediction was written down first: prefill faster,
          decode slower. Measured: <strong>prefill fell 125.7 to 121.8 tok/s and decode fell
          18.0 to 15.9</strong>. Slower on both.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          llama.cpp computes logits only for the last position of a prompt, so that tensor runs
          once per prefill rather than once per token; in decode it is bandwidth-bound and a
          heavier tensor is a straight loss. <strong>Coverage is not throughput</strong> — what
          pays is coverage of the <em>hot</em> tensors, which is why Q8_0 wins prefill by 62%
          and this rebuild lost. The file is published as a negative result and kept out of the
          catalog.
        </p>
      </CaveatCard>
      <CaveatCard title="Not an efficiency win: pinning to the LITTLE cluster">
        <p>
          The bench app's fourth arm pins Auto's thread count to the efficiency cores to ask
          whether they are actually efficient for LLM decode. Answer, on both phones measured:
          <strong>no</strong> - slower and worse tokens per watt than the performance cluster. On
          the CMF it also collapses prompt from 139 to 82.5 tok/s and pushes TTFT from 3.7 s to
          6.3 s, in exchange for the lowest raw draw (3.51 W). A real option to expose; not a
          default.
        </p>
      </CaveatCard>
      <CaveatCard title="Boundary: what the app deliberately does not do">
        <p>
          No realtime scheduling. A historical Termux experiment with
          <code>--prio 3</code> (SCHED_RR) held 4.4 tok/s under background-download contention
          where the naive config collapsed to 0.7 - a 6.3x ceiling for this class of technique -
          but <code>sched_setscheduler</code> from an unprivileged app is unreliable across OEM
          skins and can starve the UI. That CLI result is documented history, never claimed as
          app behaviour; grep <code>lib/src/main/cpp/</code> for <code>SCHED_RR</code> and find
          nothing.
        </p>
      </CaveatCard>
      <CaveatCard title="Boundary: what the backend variants do not promise">
        <p>
          Shipping seven variants does not make every model fast: KleidiAI kernels exist for
          Q4_0 and Q8_0 only, so a K-quant model misses them on any backend (see the gate above).
          And no device in the public dataset reports SME yet, so the two armv9.2 variants have
          never been the loaded one in the wild.
        </p>
      </CaveatCard>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
